.TH VEILA 1 "November 8, 2026" "Veila 0.4.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
veila \- secure Wayland screen locker
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B veila lock
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B veila idle
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B veila
.RI command " [options]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Veila uses ext-session-lock-v1 and ships one binary. Manual locking needs no
resident daemon or service. Each lock starts a per-lock supervisor and a separate
secure curtain process, which owns the surfaces, input and isolated PAM helper.
.B veila lock
stays in the foreground until unlock. For keybinds and sleep hooks use
.BR "veila lock --daemonize --wait-ready" .
.PP
After secure confirmation the supervisor handles logind, fingerprint verification,
power actions, locked suspend, live widgets and configuration reload.
If it dies, the curtain retains the lock and password authentication.
A crashed curtain is replaced with the emergency prompt and maximum password
backoff; confirmed locks retry with delays capped at five seconds.
.PP
.B veila idle
is an optional watcher for compositor idle, logind Lock and sleep events.
It starts a supervisor for each lock and reads the same TOML configuration.
Stopping or restarting the watcher does not unlock or kill an active curtain.
External idle tools can invoke the public lock command instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B lock [--daemonize] [--wait-ready]
Lock the current display. --daemonize detaches the supervisor and returns after
compositor confirmation. --wait-ready additionally waits for the rich UI on every
output. Duplicate readiness requests wait for the existing curtain and fail if
that lock ends before readiness; they do not start a second lock.
.TP
.B idle [--config=PATH] [--session-id=ID]
Run the optional idle/sleep watcher. Run one watcher per display, directly or
through veila.service. SIGHUP forces reload; invalid settings retain the last
valid configuration. Lock-before-sleep releases its inhibitor after confirmation
or logind's finite delay, so a failed lock can let suspend proceed unlocked.
.TP
.B daemon
Deprecated alias for veila idle. The legacy veilad command also warns and runs
the watcher. Both accept watcher options; --log-file is removed.
.TP
.B status
Report per-display lock state and verified supervisor, curtain and watcher
process identities. Use the desktop's WAYLAND_DISPLAY and XDG_RUNTIME_DIR.
.TP
.B reload
Request configuration reload from the live supervisor and watcher after checking
their process identities. Delivery does not imply successful configuration load.
Invalid reloads retain the running last-known-good configuration.
.TP
.B doctor
Read-only diagnostics for process records, optional watcher service, legacy
units, logind, PAM, configuration, renderer and session-lock/idle protocols.
In Hyprland it checks whether the compositor permits replacement lockers.
.TP
.B check-config
Validate configuration and theme without locking.
.TP
.B init [--theme NAME] [--force]
Create a starter configuration. --force replaces an existing file.
.TP
.B preview --preview-png=PATH
Render a PNG without locking. See veila preview --help for sizes and options.
.TP
.B logs
Show the configured lock file, when logging is enabled, and veila.service's
watcher journal. Manual locking does not require that service to be installed.
.TP
.B theme list
List bundled themes.
.TP
.B theme current
Print the active theme and resolved source.
.TP
.B theme print NAME
Print a bundled or user theme.
.TP
.B theme set NAME
Set the top-level theme key and best-effort reload running processes.
.TP
.B theme unset
Remove the top-level theme key and best-effort reload running processes.
.PP
The old stop and health commands are removed. Use systemctl to stop the watcher,
and status or doctor to inspect the session.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show help.
.TP
.B -v, --version
Print the local version.
.TP
.BI --config= PATH
Select a TOML configuration file.
.TP
.B --force-emergency-ui
Combine with lock to test the minimal secure password prompt.
.TP
.B --latency-report[=verbose]
Combine with lock --wait-ready to print startup timings. Duplicate locks do not
replay old latency measurements.
.SH LOGS OPTIONS
.TP
.B --follow, -f
Follow both selected sources until interrupted. File following tracks rotation
and retries a configured file that has not been created yet.
.TP
.B --file
Read only the configured lock log. Enable [lock].log_to_file to write future
locks; log_file_path selects its path. File logging remains opt-in.
.TP
.B --journal
Read only the systemd user journal.
.TP
.BI --since= WHEN
Read journal entries since a journalctl time or duration such as 10m or 2h.
This selects only journal entries in the default combined view; explicit --file
cannot be combined with --since.
.TP
.BI --lines= N
Limit recent lines per source (default 200, or 50 while following).
.TP
.B --idle, --curtain, --ui, --all
Select watcher, curtain, UI or all logs. --idle reads the journal only;
--daemon remains an alias for that filter. Curtain/UI filters also apply to
configured file logs. --file and --journal are mutually exclusive.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is TOML. Veila reads an explicit --config path first, then the user
config at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/veila/config.toml (normally ~/.config/veila/config.toml),
then /etc/veila/config.toml. Missing initial settings use safe defaults;
.B veila init
creates a starter file. Existing themes and visual keys remain supported.
.PP
[idle].enabled defaults to false. When the watcher runs, it locks after
lock_after_seconds (default 300, clamped to 5..86400) through the inhibitor-aware
ext-idle-notify-v1 protocol. lock_before_sleep defaults to true independently
of idle timeout. External idle tools configure their own timeout and sleep hooks.
.PP
[lock].log_to_file defaults to false. The default log_file_path is
~/.local/state/veila/veila.log. Foreground diagnostics also go to stderr;
detached lock diagnostics require file logging.
.SH FONTS
Bundled fonts provide initial password text. After confirmation, a bounded
Fontconfig query selects configured and script fallback faces off the Wayland
thread. Missing, oversized or slow optional fonts retain the bundled fallback.
Install fontconfig and fonts covering the scripts you use.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/veila/config.toml
User configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/veila/themes/
User themes.
.TP
.I /usr/share/veila/
Bundled themes, fonts, icons and example units (prefix varies by installation).
.TP
.I /etc/pam.d/veila
Password authentication service.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/veila.service
Optional watcher unit running veila idle. KillMode=process preserves independent
locks across watcher restarts.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/veilad.service
Temporary compatibility alias where packaged. Migrate old enablement links
as reported by veila doctor. Nix exposes the current veila name only.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VEILA_ASSET_DIR
Override the packaged assets directory.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Control tracing verbosity.
.SH SECURITY
Only ext-session-lock-v1 provides secure coverage. A regular fullscreen window is
not a lock. The curtain unlocks only for its current accepted PAM attempt or a
supervisor command from verified fingerprint/session logind Unlock. Signals,
render failures, configuration errors and process disconnections never authorize
unlock. Direct internal __curtain development mode has no recovery supervisor.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Lock manually:
.B veila lock
.TP
Sleep hook or compositor keybind:
.B veila lock --daemonize --wait-ready
.TP
Optional automatic locking:
.B systemctl --user enable --now veila.service
.TP
Inspect watcher logs only:
.B veila logs --journal --idle
.TP
Follow configured lock logs:
.B veila logs --file --follow
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR pam (8)
